---
/* ExponentialOS, a computer built for agents (2026-10-31). The drafts are
   the stills of apps/marketing's HTML drawings; the chat is painted by genUI. */
import { Aside } from "@astrojs/starlight/components"
import BlogPost from "../../components/landing/BlogPost.astro"
import { DESKTOP_CHAT_SURFACE } from "../../components/landing/blog-surfaces.ts"
import EuiSurface from "../../components/eui/EuiSurface.tsx"

const PARTS: [string, string][] = [
  [`Linux`, `The kernel, drivers and systemd. Everything that runs on Linux runs here.`],
  [`Compositor`, `Owns the screen and every input. Gives you a seat and each agent its own.`],
  [`Services`, `Files, network, packages, settings, devices: each one an MCP server.`],
  [`MCP Proxy`, `The glue between parts: serves tools, grants them per agent, writes an audit row per call.`],
  [`Core`, `The types every part shares: ids, grants, results, events. Nothing else.`],
  [`Agents`, `Runs your agent and the agents it starts, wakes them on schedules and events.`],
  [`Computer Use`, `Computer use: sees and drives windows in an agent's own seat.`],
  [`Voice`, `Voice in and out: wake word, speech to text, text to speech.`],
  [`genUI`, `Every screen as data, painted natively. The desktop is made of it.`],
  [`P2P`, `Your devices, connected directly to each other.`],
]
---

<BlogPost slug="exponentialos">
  <h2 id="picture">The whole picture</h2>
  <p>
    ExponentialOS is Linux with agents built in. Each layer does one job, and the parts talk to each other through tools. Follow one spoken request down the stack: it reaches an agent, the agent
    calls computer use through the glue, and the click lands in the agent's own seat.
  </p>
  <p>One request, six steps: what you say, Voice, Agents, MCP Proxy, Computer Use, an agent's seat in the compositor. ExponentialOS is planned; the figures in this post are drafts.</p>

  <h2 id="seats">Every agent gets its own seat</h2>
  <p>
    The compositor decides where every key and click goes. You get a seat with the real keyboard, mouse and monitors; each agent gets a virtual one and an offscreen space. Agents work while you play,
    and your pointer never moves. A window an agent borrows stays on your panel as a live tile.
  </p>
  <img src="/blog/agent-seats.webp" width="1392" height="712" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Two routes through one compositor: yours to your monitor, the agent's to its own space (draft)" />

  <h2 id="data">The desktop is data</h2>
  <p>Panel, menu, notifications and settings are genUI documents on disk. An agent changes them like any file: each edit is checked, appears live and can be undone.</p>
  <img src="/blog/ui-edit.webp" width="1392" height="526" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="“Put the weather in the tray”: one line in the panel's file, checked, live, a commit (draft)" />

  <h2 id="agent">Your agent, one key away</h2>
  <p>
    <kbd>Super Space</kbd> or your voice opens a chat with your agent. It is one conversation that never ends: it starts other agents for longer work, sleeps, and wakes when they report or a schedule fires.
  </p>
  <div class="exp-live">
    <EuiSurface id="desktop-chat" tree={DESKTOP_CHAT_SURFACE} />
  </div>
  <img src="/blog/agent-orchestra.webp" width="1392" height="372" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="A schedule wakes your agent, it starts a flow, the flow's report wakes it again (draft)" />

  <h2 id="parts">The parts</h2>
  <dl>
    {
      PARTS.map(([name, text]) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{name}</dt>
          <dd>{text}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>
  <p>Each module works on its own in any project; together they are the system.</p>

  <h2 id="today">What exists today</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>genUI:</strong> shipped. Six renderers, one conformance suite.</li>
    <li><strong>Agents, Computer Use, Voice, P2P:</strong> shipped inside Exponential, released as SDK modules.</li>
    <li><strong>MCP Proxy:</strong> in progress. The contract and the server half.</li>
    <li><strong>The compositor, the desktop, Core:</strong> planned. The figures above are drafts.</li>
  </ul>
